		emoji = "🌬️",
		source = "wikipedia",
		description = "Conversion of dinitrogen into ammonia",
		tags = { "More articles" },
		summary = "**Nitrogen fixation** is a chemical process by which the abundant but relatively inert molecular dinitrogen (N2) is converted into bioavailable nitrogen compounds such as [[Ammonia|ammonia]] (NH3) and [[Nitrate|nitrates]] (NO3). It occurs both biologically and abiologically, the latter either naturally through weather phenomena (e.g. [[Lightning|lightning]]) or artificially by [[Chemical industry|chemical industries]]. Biological nitrogen fixation or diazotrophy is catalyzed by [[Enzyme|enzymes]] called nitrogenases, produced by [[Microorganism|microorganisms]] such as [[Cyanobacteria|cyanobacteria]] and rhizobia. These enzyme complexes are encoded by the Nif genes (or Nif homologs) and contain [[Iron|iron]], often with a second metal (usually [[Molybdenum|molybdenum]], but sometimes [[Vanadium|vanadium]]).",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Some nitrogen-fixing bacteria have symbiotic relationships with [[Plant|plants]], especially [[Legume|legumes]], [[Moss|mosses]], and aquatic ferns such as Azolla. Looser non-symbiotic relationships between diazotrophs and plants are often referred to as associative, as seen in nitrogen fixation on [[Rice|rice]] roots. Nitrogen fixation occurs between some [[Termite|termites]] and [[Mushroom|fungi]]. It occurs naturally in the air by means of NOx production by [[Lightning|lightning]]. Nitrogen fixation is a key component of the nitrogen cycle and is essential to [[Life|life]] on [[Earth]], as all vital [[Organic compound|organic compounds]] such as [[DNA]] and [[Protein|proteins]] contain nitrogen. Industrial nitrogen fixation, most notably through the [[Haber process]], underpins the manufacture of all nitrogenous industrial products, which include [[Fertilizer|fertilizers]], pharmaceuticals, [[Textile|textiles]], [[Dye|dyes]] and explosives.",
